-- allium: 3
--
-- Biscuit Games — operation
--
-- Scope
--   How every Biscuit Games surface is worked: reached and invoked from a
--   keyboard, sized and spaced for a finger, acknowledged when it is touched,
--   and told to a reader who is not looking at it. appearance.allium says how a
--   surface looks; this says how it is operated.
--
-- Includes
--   - Keyboard operability of every operation a surface provides, a focus
--     indication that arrives without being asked for, and what a surface owes
--     when it replaces the control a reader is standing on.
--   - What a dialog owes: focus in when it opens, Escape out, Tab held inside,
--     and focus returned to whatever opened it.
--   - The comfortable target and the narrowest supported width, the two shapes
--     that cannot meet the target in both directions and what each does
--     instead, and where a control meets the figure in the label that
--     activates it rather than in its own box.
--   - Touch: a tap doing the control's action and nothing else, deliberate zoom
--     kept, and a touch acknowledged from the moment of contact.
--   - A change nobody is looking at being said in words.
--   - What a surface owes when it claims bare key presses for itself, and what
--     dropping that claim has to leave behind.
--
-- Excludes
--   - Colour, contrast, motion and the legibility floors. appearance.allium
--     states those, and an unavailable control's exemption from them is stated
--     there too.
--   - Which operations a surface has, and what any of them do. This module
--     states that an operation is reachable; it never states what the
--     operations are. Enter meaning "submit this word" is a game's.
--   - Markup, event handling, focus-management mechanics, and how a dialog is
--     drawn. This module says what is owed, never what listens.
--   - What a play surface's marks are and how far apart they sit.
--     play-surfaces.allium states those, and states on-screen keys as a surface
--     made of them; the size of one of those keys and its place in its row are
--     this module's, under EveryControlIsAComfortableTarget.
--   - How a surface binds a label to the control it names. The invariant says
--     which shape the platform answers for and which the surface answers for
--     itself, and says nothing about how either is written.
--   - Where the switch is that stops a surface claiming bare keys, and whether
--     there is one. A control that changes a setting belongs to the product
--     that owns it, as it does in appearance.allium; the open question at the
--     end records what is still undecided about that.
--
-- Dependencies
--   None. This is one of the platform's root modules, and it imports nothing so
--   that a game can inherit these obligations whole rather than restate them.
--   appearance.allium and play-surfaces.allium are the others; none of the
--   three imports another, because they are peers describing different aspects
--   of the same surfaces rather than layers.

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Given
------------------------------------------------------------

given {
    -- How wide the surface has to lay out in, in CSS pixels. The device decides
    -- it and Biscuit Games only ever lays out to it.
    viewport_width: Integer

    -- Whether the surface in front of the reader is claiming bare key presses
    -- for itself.
    --
    -- Something outside this module decides it — a game's setting, a mode, a
    -- surface that claims only while it is being played — and this module
    -- deliberately never says which. It is not config, because it changes while
    -- the reader is there rather than being a fixed threshold; it is not a
    -- stored setting, because nothing here stores one; and it is not left
    -- entirely to a game, because the obligations that attach to claiming and
    -- to dropping the claim are the platform's and need something to be stated
    -- against.
    typed_keys_are_claimed: Boolean
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Config
------------------------------------------------------------

config {
    -- CSS pixels across a control, in both directions, for the invariant that
    -- names it. A dense row of like controls is the one place this cannot be
    -- met in both, which is why that invariant says what happens instead rather
    -- than stating a size alone.
    minimum_touch_target: Integer = 44

    -- CSS pixels. The narrowest viewport every promise in this module survives
    -- without scrolling sideways, and the width every target and spacing figure
    -- has to hold at.
    narrowest_supported_width: Integer = 320
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Contracts
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Every surface with a control a reader can tap fulfils this, which is why it
-- lives here rather than on any one of them. Touch is the primary way these
-- games are played, and a gesture the platform interprets for itself is a
-- gesture that did not reach the game.
contract DirectManipulation {
    @invariant ATapDoesOnlyWhatTheControlDoes
        -- A tap on a control performs that control's action and nothing
        -- besides. The label is not selected, no callout or magnifier is raised
        -- by holding, and tapping the same control twice in quick succession
        -- performs it twice rather than zooming the page. Repeated fast tapping
        -- on a row of keys is ordinary use, not a gesture.

    @invariant DeliberateZoomIsNeverTakenAway
        -- What the previous invariant removes is the platform's guess, never
        -- the reader's intent. Pinch-zoom still magnifies the page, and the
        -- page stays usable when the operating system or the browser is already
        -- magnifying it. Refusing to be zoomed at all would satisfy the first
        -- invariant and fail this one; the first is not licence for it.

    @invariant EveryControlIsAComfortableTarget
        -- Every control is at least config.minimum_touch_target across, in both
        -- directions. Two shapes cannot be. The first is a row of like controls
        -- sharing the full width, where what the row would need is more room
        -- than it is given: the figure for every control that builds a turn,
        -- the gaps between them all, and whatever more a control that ends a
        -- turn is given over one that builds one.
        --
        -- Three terms, and each was left out of a wrong version of this clause.
        -- How many controls are in the row is not the test on its own: seven
        -- controls at that figure fit inside config.narrowest_supported_width
        -- and the six gaps between them do not, so a row this invariant
        -- declined to exempt could not meet the figure either — a rack of seven
        -- tiles is the shape that found it. The room is what the row is
        -- actually given rather than the whole viewport, because a row inside a
        -- page's margins has less than the screen it is drawn on. And the extra
        -- a turn-ending control takes is counted rather than assumed away: the
        -- clause below grants it, so a row carrying two of them has that much
        -- less to divide among the rest, and leaving the term out declared a
        -- ten-share row unexempted at a width that renders it below the figure.
        --
        -- There the row meets the figure top to bottom, divides its width
        -- equally among the controls that build a turn, and keeps a gap between
        -- them, so what such a control gives up is bounded by the width of the
        -- screen and by nothing else. A control in that row that ends a turn
        -- rather than builds one may be wider than the others, and never
        -- narrower: those are the last place to save width.
        --
        -- The second is a control inside a line of running text, a link in a
        -- sentence being the one that occurs: its size is set by the text around
        -- it, and a control given the figure there would break the line it sits
        -- in. A surface owes such a control a reading distance from the words
        -- beside it, and owes it nothing else. Where the same destination is
        -- also offered away from the prose, that offer meets the figure like any
        -- other control.
        --
        -- Every other control meets the figure outright, and a control the
        -- reader activates by pressing a label that contains it meets the figure
        -- in that label rather than in its own box: the label is what the finger
        -- is aimed at, so the label is what has to answer, and the words beside
        -- the control are part of the target rather than next to it.
        --
        -- A label bound to a control it does not contain is the surface's own to
        -- size. The platform cannot see that binding from the label — it is a
        -- name matched against an identifier elsewhere in the document — so a
        -- surface that binds that way answers for the row itself. Stated rather
        -- than left out, because the shape is legal, common, and would otherwise
        -- fall through the figure with nothing saying whose it was.
        --
        -- A control a surface builds out of a generic element is the surface's
        -- own to size for the same reason: an element the platform cannot tell
        -- from ordinary content is one no shared rule can reach. What the
        -- platform answers for is what it can name — every native control the
        -- two shapes above do not exempt, and the label that contains one — and
        -- it answers for all of those rather than for the few a stylesheet
        -- happened to list. A link in a sentence is named as easily as any of
        -- them and is still exempt, because the exemption is about where the
        -- control sits rather than about whether it can be reached.
        --
        -- All of this holds down to config.narrowest_supported_width, at which
        -- the surface is usable without scrolling sideways.

    @invariant ATouchIsAcknowledged
        -- A control shows that it was touched, from the moment of contact,
        -- whatever the platform's own tap feedback is doing. Suppressing that
        -- feedback without replacing it leaves a control that reads as dead
        -- under exactly the finger this contract exists for. The
        -- acknowledgement is a change the eye catches without motion, so it owes
        -- nothing to whether animations are running — and it is not a dimming of
        -- the control, because a control that carries a mark would lose the mark
        -- along with the light.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Surfaces
------------------------------------------------------------

-- What every Biscuit Games surface owes to being worked, whatever it is for.
surface Operation {
    let within_supported_width = viewport_width >= config.narrowest_supported_width

    exposes:
        within_supported_width

    contracts:
        fulfils DirectManipulation

    @guarantee FullyKeyboardOperable
        -- Every operation a surface provides can be reached and invoked from
        -- the keyboard alone, and the state of the control focus lands on is
        -- reported as well as drawn. This holds whatever else the surface is
        -- doing with the keyboard: a surface that claims bare keys for itself
        -- claims none that a control already needs, which is TypedInput below.

    @guarantee FocusIsVisibleWhereverItLands
        -- Focus is drawn on every control that can take it, in an indication a
        -- control inherits rather than asks for, so a control cannot be added
        -- without one. A control that draws no boundary of its own still shows
        -- focus.

    @guarantee AControlThatDisappearsHandsFocusOn
        -- A surface that replaces the control the reader is standing on carries
        -- focus across the swap. Nothing downstream can repair this after the
        -- fact: a removed element reports no departure, so focus falls to the
        -- page and a reader who was working by keyboard resumes from the top.
        -- The obligation is the replacing surface's, at the moment it replaces.

    @guarantee AChangeNobodyIsLookingAtIsAnnounced
        -- A surface that changes something without moving focus says so in
        -- words, and says it again when it happens again — the same thing twice
        -- over is heard twice. The platform states that an announcement is owed
        -- and what carries it; which sentence is said belongs to whatever had
        -- something to say.

    @guarantee EveryPromiseHereHoldsAtTheNarrowestWidth
        -- Every clause in this module holds down to
        -- config.narrowest_supported_width, at which nothing scrolls sideways
        -- and no operation has become unreachable. Above it there is more room
        -- and nothing to relax.
}

-- A panel that takes the reader's whole attention while it is open. Stated here
-- rather than in a game because the shell is the platform's, and a contract for
-- a shared component's keyboard behaviour that lived in one game's
-- specification would be the copy governing the original.
surface Dialog {
    exposes:
        within_supported_width

    contracts:
        fulfils DirectManipulation

    @guarantee FocusEntersTheDialogWhenItOpens
        -- Opening a dialog puts focus inside its panel rather than leaving it
        -- behind on the page.

    @guarantee EscapeClosesTheDialog
        -- Escape closes it, from anywhere inside it, and closing is the same act
        -- however it is reached.

    @guarantee TabIsHeldInsideTheDialog
        -- Tab cycles over the stops the panel's contents really make and never
        -- wanders out to what is behind. A dialog that has declared itself modal
        -- over the page and then lets Tab leave has broken the declaration. The
        -- stops are the ones the keyboard genuinely lands on, which is not the
        -- same as everything that looks like a control: a group of exclusive
        -- choices is one stop rather than several, a control the layout does not
        -- draw is none at all, and a disclosure's own summary is one that is
        -- easily missed.

    @guarantee FocusReturnsToWhateverOpenedIt
        -- On the way out, focus goes back to the control that opened the dialog
        -- if it is still on the page. This is the clause easiest to leave out
        -- and invisible until someone tries it.
}

-- Bare key presses reaching a surface directly, rather than reaching whichever
-- control has focus. The one channel a surface can decline entirely.
surface TypedInput {
    -- A surface that is not claiming keys is indistinguishable, from the
    -- keyboard's side, from one that never could.
    let keys_reach_the_reader = not typed_keys_are_claimed

    exposes:
        keys_reach_the_reader

    @guarantee AClaimNeverReachesAFocusedControl
        -- While focus is on a control, that control's own keys stay its own:
        -- the key that activates it activates it, and somewhere the reader is
        -- typing keeps every key. A claim on bare keys is a claim on keys
        -- nothing else has asked for.

    @guarantee AModifiedKeyIsNeverClaimed
        -- A key pressed together with a shortcut modifier — Control, Meta or
        -- Alt — belongs to the browser and the operating system, and is never
        -- taken. Those three and not every modifier the keyboard has: Shift
        -- carries no shortcut of its own on any platform this runs on, so a
        -- shifted letter is still the reader typing a letter and a surface that
        -- claims letters claims that one. A surface deciding what its alphabet
        -- is is free to case-fold or not; this clause says only that Shift is
        -- not what takes a key away from it.
        --
        -- The test is the shortcut and not the composing. Alt composes as well
        -- as it addresses — on macOS it is the compose key, where a reader types
        -- a Nordic or accented letter with it — and it is on the list anyway,
        -- because it also carries shortcuts a surface must not swallow. The cost
        -- is real and named here rather than discovered: a game whose alphabet
        -- includes a letter composed with Alt does not receive it as a bare key
        -- press, and reaches it through a control the reader can operate.

    @guarantee DroppingTheClaimIsTotal
        -- Not claiming means not intercepting, rather than intercepting and
        -- then ignoring. A surface that has dropped the claim handles no key
        -- press at all, so bare keys reach the reader's own software and the key
        -- that activates a control activates whichever control has focus. This
        -- is what the claim is worth stopping for: a reader who navigates by
        -- single letter loses that navigation entirely to an application that
        -- swallows those keys, and only they can know they are doing it.

    @guarantee TypedKeysAnswerTheSameOperationsAsTheKeysOnScreen
        -- Where a surface offers an operation both through a claimed key and
        -- through a key drawn on screen, the two are the same operation under
        -- the same name. Which operations those are is not this module's to
        -- say.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open Questions
------------------------------------------------------------

-- That a surface which claims bare keys owes a way to stop is not the same as
-- owing the reader a switch. The accessibility case for one is strong: a reader
-- who navigates by single letter is the person the claim costs, and only they
-- know they are doing it. But appearance.allium excludes the controls that
-- change a setting on the ground that a panel belongs to the product that owns
-- it, and this module follows it — so the platform can require that the claim be
-- droppable without being able to say who offers the reader the means. Nobody
-- has decided which of the two it is, and answering it in code would let
-- whichever repository draws a settings panel first decide it.
open question "Dropping the claim - does the platform owe the reader a way to stop a surface claiming bare keys, or only that dropping it costs nothing?"
